    rhythmSrc: "No reliable account of his daily routine was found.",
    practices: [
      { id: "trowel", cat: "persuasion", name: "Flattery as method", stage: "Throughout; above all with the Queen",
        what: "Treated flattery as a deliberate tool and said so: he told Matthew Arnold that everyone liked it and that with royalty it should be laid on with a trowel.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "Victoria, lonely after Albert's death, responded to his warmth and courtliness, and became his political ally.",
        cost: "Gladstone and others saw it as insincerity; it worked on those who wanted it to.",
        tryit: "Genuine, specific appreciation opens doors. Make it true, or it will be seen for what it is.",
        attrib: ["self"], grade: "reported", source: "G. W. E. Russell, Collections and Recollections (1898)" },
      { id: "queenletters", cat: "network", name: "Letters to the Queen in his best novel style", stage: "1868 and 1874–1880",
        what: "As prime minister he wrote to Victoria constantly, with gossip, colour and parliamentary narrative; the Earl of Clarendon wrote in 1868 that 'Dizzy writes daily letters to the Queen in his best novel style'.",
        volume: "Near-daily letters",
        mechanism: "He turned the dull duty of reporting to the sovereign into a relationship.",
        cost: "Critics thought he drew the monarch into partisan politics.",
        tryit: "Make your regular reports to the people above you worth reading — vivid, frank and short.",
        attrib: ["witness"], grade: "documented", source: "Clarendon to Lady Salisbury, 26 June 1868, in Maxwell, Life of Clarendon (1913)" },
      { id: "bradford", cat: "network", name: "A thousand letters to Lady Bradford", stage: "1873–1881",
        what: "In his last years he wrote more than a thousand letters to Selina, Countess of Bradford, and hundreds to her sister Lady Chesterfield — confiding political news and his moods.",
        volume: "1,000+ letters",
        mechanism: "Writing to a confidante was how he thought aloud and kept his spirits up.",
        cost: "Indiscretion: the letters were full of cabinet secrets.",
        tryit: "Have one person outside work you can think aloud with in writing — and keep the confidential out of it.",
        attrib: ["witness", "biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Oxford DNB; Zetland (ed.), Letters of Disraeli to Lady Bradford and Lady Chesterfield (1929)" },
      { id: "maiden", cat: "prep", name: "After the failed maiden speech", stage: "1837",
        what: "His florid maiden speech in December 1837 was laughed down. He took advice, reported in his letters, from the Irish MP Richard Lalor Sheil: speak often but briefly, be quiet and even dull for a while, and let the House get used to him.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "He changed his style to suit the audience instead of insisting on his own.",
        cost: "A long apprenticeship: he did not hold office until 1852.",
        tryit: "When a first performance fails, ask the audience's veterans what they want — and adapt.",
        attrib: ["self", "biographer"], grade: "reported", source: "Disraeli's letters to his sister (Dec 1837), in Monypenny & Buckle; Hansard 7 Dec 1837" },
      { id: "hughenden", cat: "patrons", name: "Borrowing a country seat", stage: "1848",
        what: "To lead a party of landowners he needed to be one. He bought Hughenden Manor with a loan of £25,000 from the brothers of his patron Lord George Bentinck.",
        volume: "£25,000 loan",
        mechanism: "The estate gave an outsider the standing the Conservative backbenches demanded of their leaders.",
        cost: "Debt that followed him for decades.",
        tryit: "Know which credentials your audience requires, and invest in them — carefully.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Blake, Disraeli" },
      { id: "corry", cat: "machine", name: "Montagu Corry", stage: "1866–1881",
        what: "Kept the same private secretary, Montagu Corry, for fifteen years — unpaid during the years in opposition — who managed his papers, his diary and his health and became his literary executor.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "A devoted secretary freed him for politics and society.",
        cost: "None obvious.",
        tryit: "One excellent assistant, kept for years, is worth more than a rotating staff.",
        attrib: ["witness", "biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Blake, Disraeli" },
      { id: "machine", cat: "machine", name: "Organising for a mass electorate", stage: "1867–1874",
        what: "After the 1867 Reform Act enlarged the electorate, he backed the creation of the National Union of Conservative associations (1867) and made John Gorst the party's principal agent (1870), with a Central Office organising candidates and constituencies — the machinery behind the 1874 victory.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "The party that organised the new voters first won them.",
        cost: "He neglected the organisation afterwards, and it decayed before the 1880 defeat.",
        tryit: "When your market changes, rebuild the machinery to reach it — and keep maintaining it after you win.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Blake, Disraeli; histories of Conservative Central Office" }
    ],
    books: ["disraeli-blake", "hurd-young-disraeli"]
