  cavour: {
    oneLine: "An agronomist, banker and newspaper founder who studied how England governed, then ran Piedmont by holding several ministries at once, building a parliamentary coalition behind his prime minister's back, and using every channel to Napoleon III.",
    ownWords: [
      { text: "I have even tried to stimulate the patriotism of the beautiful Castiglione, so that she may seduce the emperor.", src: "Letter to Urbano Rattazzi, 22 February 1856 (translated)" },
      { text: "He called his secretary Costantino Nigra perhaps the only person who knew all his thoughts, even the most secret.", src: "Paraphrase, as quoted in biographies of Nigra", para: true }
    ],
    rhythm: [],
    rhythmSrc: "His daily hours are not reliably documented in the sources checked.",
    practices: [
      { id: "studytour", cat: "prep", name: "Studying England", stage: "1835 and after",
        what: "Spent six months in 1835 travelling through Geneva, Paris, London, Belgium and the Rhineland studying poor relief, prisons and railways and attending parliamentary debates; he took Robert Peel as a model and kept diaries from 1833.",
        volume: "Six months, five countries",
        mechanism: "He arrived in politics with a working model of liberal parliamentary government and modern economics that his rivals lacked.",
        cost: "It made him seem too English and too French to some Piedmontese.",
        tryit: "Go and see how the best in your field actually operate, take notes, and come back with a model.",
        attrib: ["self", "biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Treccani, Dizionario Biografico; Mack Smith, Cavour" },
      { id: "leri", cat: "prep", name: "The estate as a laboratory", stage: "1835–1850",
        what: "Ran his family's estate at Leri as an experiment — new livestock, chemical fertilisers, machinery — raising its output, co-founded an agricultural association, invested in railways and helped found a bank. He also lost money speculating.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "Practical business experience gave him a command of economics and finance unusual among politicians.",
        cost: "Losses on speculation in 1840 taught the hard way.",
        tryit: "Run something real with your own money before you advise others on theirs.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Treccani; Mack Smith, Cavour" },
      { id: "newspaper", cat: "persuasion", name: "Founding Il Risorgimento", stage: "1847",
        what: "Co-founded and drove the newspaper Il Risorgimento, first published in December 1847, which gave his moderate liberal programme a voice and a name to the national movement.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "Owning a platform let him set the terms of debate before he held office.",
        cost: "Journalism made him enemies on both left and right.",
        tryit: "Publish your thinking regularly; a body of work does more than a reputation.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Treccani" },
      { id: "connubio", cat: "network", name: "The connubio", stage: "1852",
        what: "Without telling his prime minister, Massimo d'Azeglio, he struck a deal with the centre-left under Urbano Rattazzi — the 'marriage' (connubio) — sealed by his speech of 5 February 1852, creating the parliamentary majority that made him prime minister that November.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "A centre coalition isolated both extremes and gave him a durable majority.",
        cost: "D'Azeglio's government fell; trust was the price.",
        tryit: "Build alliances across the middle before you need them — and be open with your own side about it.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Treccani; Mack Smith, Cavour" },
      { id: "ministries", cat: "machine", name: "Holding the ministries himself", stage: "1850–1861",
        what: "Combined offices to an extraordinary degree: agriculture and navy, then finance, then as prime minister also foreign affairs, the interior, and at times war and the navy.",
        volume: "Up to four ministries at once",
        mechanism: "Concentrating the key departments let him coordinate economic, diplomatic and military policy personally.",
        cost: "Overwork; he died in 1861, aged fifty, worn out.",
        tryit: "Know which few functions you must control personally — and be honest about the cost to your health.",
        attrib: ["biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Treccani" },
      { id: "castiglione", cat: "persuasion", name: "Every channel to Napoleon III", stage: "1855–1858",
        what: "Worked on the French emperor through every channel he could — his secretary Costantino Nigra in Paris, the emperor's doctor, and, as his letters of February 1856 show, his cousin the Countess of Castiglione, whom he encouraged to seduce Napoleon in Piedmont's cause. The often-quoted 'succeed, cousin, by any means' is not attested.",
        volume: null,
        mechanism: "Multiple informal channels secured the French alliance agreed at Plombières in 1858.",
        cost: "The exploitation of the countess is the dark side of the method.",
        tryit: "Not one to copy in its means. The neutral point is to have more than one route to the decision-maker.",
        attrib: ["self", "biographer"], grade: "documented", source: "Cavour's letters to Cibrario and Rattazzi, February 1856" }
    ],
    books: ["cavour-mack-smith", "hearder-cavour"]
  },

